   296	## 5. Task (1): the nodal collapse at general `d = N`
   297	
   298	### 5.1 The crude collapse, and exactly how far it goes
   299	
   300	"Nodal" in the charge means `nu = s`, i.e. **every singular point has exactly
   301	two branches**. (It therefore also covers tacnodes, ordinary tangencies and all
   302	`A_{2k+1}`; I flag below where genuine smoothness of the branches is needed.)
   303	With `nu = s`, `(M'-def)` is
   304	
   305	```text
   306	(5.1)   sum_{p ∈ Sing A_F} (a - a_p) = (a - 1) + s(N - a),   0 <= a_p <= a.
   307	```
   308	
   309	`LHS <= s·a`, so a solution needs `s·a >= (a-1) + s(N-a)`, i.e.
   310	
   311	```text
   312	(5.2)   s·(2a - N) >= a - 1.
   313	```
   314	
   315	Exact case analysis, `1 <= a <= N-2`, `N >= 3`, and `s >= 1` by SMOOTH-KILL:
   316	
   317	* `2a <= N`. If `a >= 2` then `LHS <= 0 < a-1 = RHS`: impossible. If `a = 1`
   318	  then `(5.2)` reads `s(2 - N) >= 0`, i.e. `s(N-2) <= 0`; `N >= 3` forces
   319	  `s = 0`, contradicting SMOOTH-KILL. **No solution.**
   320	* `2a > N`. `(5.2)` holds for `s >= (a-1)/(2a-N)`, so solutions exist
   321	  numerically. But `a <= N-2` and `2a > N` require `N/2 < a <= N-2`, hence
   322	  `N > 4`.
   323	
   324	> **PROPOSITION 5.1.** Under `H2`, with at least one multibranch point:
   325	> (i) at `N = 3` this is impossible (`(C1)` gives `2a >= 3` while
   326	> `a <= N-2 = 1`); (ii) at `N = 4` it forces `a = 2`, `W = 2`, `D_gap = 0`, so
   327	> `K_p = 0` at every multibranch `p`, and `(K)` puts the whole excess
   328	> `a - 1 = 1` on **unibranch** points: `A_F` must have a **cusp**. In
   329	> particular there is no nodal `A_F` at `N ∈ {3,4}`, and more generally no
   330	> `A_F` all of whose singular points are multibranch.
   331	
   332	Proposition 5.1 subsumes REP-96 §7 (R4) at `N = 4`: R4 kills nodal `A_F` there
   333	for `s >= 1`; SMOOTH-KILL closes `s = 0`; `(C1)` upgrades "nodal" to
   334	"every singular point multibranch". The `a = d-2` boundary is not hand-waved: at
   335	`N = 4` it is `a = 2` and dies because `s(2a-N) = 0 < a-1 = 1`; at general `N`
   336	it dies inside the `2a > N` branch below. But `LHS <= s·a` is **not** enough for `N >= 5` — the
   337	`2a > N` branch is genuinely non-empty for `(5.2)` — and the sharpening comes
   338	from `(L)`, `(K)`, `(C3)` and 7.B.
   339	
   340	### 5.2 The sharp form: all branches smooth
   341	
   342	Assume every branch of `A_F` is smooth. Then a singular point cannot be
   343	unibranch, so `r_p >= 2` for all `p ∈ Sing D`, `beta = 0`, and `nu >= s` (nodal
   344	is the case `nu = s`). By `(C1)`, `K_p <= D_gap := 2a - N` for every `p`; by
   345	`(C3)`, at most `R` points have `K_p > 0`; by `(K)` their total is `a - 1`. So
   346	
   347	```text
   348	(5.3)   a - 1 <= R · D_gap ,     D_gap = 2a - N,   R = sum_l (s_l - 1).
   349	```
   350	
   351	If `a = 1` then `(K)` is `0 = 0`, but `(C1)` gives `2 >= 2a >= N >= 3`:
   352	impossible. So `a >= 2`, `D_gap >= 1`. Now 7.B enters through `R`: since every
   353	`mu_l >= 2` and `sum_l s_l mu_l = W`, we have `sum_l s_l <= floor(W/2)` and
   354	`R = sum_l s_l - m <= floor(W/2) - 1` (`m >= 1`). Writing `R_max(W)` for the
   355	exact maximum of `R` over dicritical profiles of weight `W` with all `mu_l >= 2`
   356	(`= W/2 - 1` for `W` even; `max(W/p_min(W) - 1, (W-5)/2)` for `W` odd), the
   357	system to solve is
   358	
   359	```text
   360	(5.4)   a - 1 <= (2a - N)·R_max(N - a),    N/2 < a <= N - 2,   N - a >= 2.
   361	```
   362	
   363	Using the clean bound `R <= W/2 - 1` and `a = (N + D_gap)/2`, `W = (N-D_gap)/2`,
   364	`(5.4)` becomes `2(N + D_gap - 2) <= D_gap(N - D_gap - 4)`, i.e.
   365	
   366	```text
   367	(5.5)   N·(D_gap - 2) >= D_gap^2 + 6 D_gap - 4.
   368	```
   369	
   370	`D_gap = 1` gives `-N >= 11`, `D_gap = 2` gives `0 >= 12`: both impossible, so
   371	`D_gap >= 3`; then `D_gap = 3 ⟹ N >= 23`, `4 ⟹ N >= 18`, `5 ⟹ N >= 17`,
   372	`6 ⟹ N >= 17`, `7,8 ⟹ N >= 18`, and the right side of `(5.5)` divided by
   373	`D_gap - 2` is increasing for `D_gap >= 6`. Parity (`D_gap ≡ N mod 2`) removes
   374	`D_gap = 6` at `N = 17`.
   375	
   376	> **THEOREM NODAL-ALL-N.** Let `F` be a noninvertible plane Keller map of
   377	> geometric degree `N` with `A_F` irreducible, and suppose every branch of
   378	> `A_F` is smooth (in particular, `A_F` nodal). Then **`N >= 17`**, and the
   379	> admissible data are exactly the solutions of `(5.4)`. At `N = 17` the unique
   380	> solution saturates both inequalities:
   381	> `a = 11, W = 6, D_gap = 5, m = 1, (s_1,mu_1) = (3,2), R = 2`, with
   382	> `sum_p K_p = 10` concentrated as `K = 5` at exactly two singular points, each
   383	> then having `r_p = 2` and `a_p = 0`; every other singular point has `r_p = 2`,
   384	> `K_p = 0`, `a_p = 5`; `s >= 2`.
