    98	*Proof.* `{T,U}=-A^{-2}{A,U}=-2`, so `{T,V}={T,UT}=T{T,U}=-2T`. Consistency:
    99	`{V,Z}={V,V^2-T}=-{V,T}=-2T`, which agrees with the direct reduction of
   100	`{U,Z}=2+4AZ`. QED
   101	
   102	Two arithmetic checks against the generators, done by hand and both exact:
   103	with `f=A, g=U` formula (2.2) returns `2T^-2=2A^2`; with `f=U, g=Z` it returns
   104	`4V^2T^-1-2 = 2+4AZ`. So (2.2) is the promoted bracket, not a renaming.
   105	
   106	Write `v` for the `T`-adic valuation on `C[T^{\pm},V]` and expand
   107	`f=sum_i f_i(V) T^i`. Formula (2.2) gives the exact coefficient law
   108	
   109	```text
   110	[T^m]{f,g} = 2 * sum_{i+j=m} ( j f_i' g_j - i f_i g_j' ),  ' = d/dV.  (2.3)
   111	```
   112	
   113	Let `sigma: V -> -V`; it fixes `T` and is exactly the Galois involution
   114	`U -> -U`, and it is anti-Poisson.
   115	
   116	**Proposition 2.3 (degree dictionary).** For `f=P+UQ` in `RR`:
   117	`v(P) = -deg_A P` and `v(UQ) = -1-deg_A Q`, and these are the even and odd
   118	parts of `f` under `sigma`. Consequently, for every `d>=0`,
   119	
   120	```text
   121	deg_A P <= d and deg_A Q <= d   <=>   v(f) >= -(d+1) and f_{-(d+1)} is odd.
   122	```
   123	
   124	In particular the `A`-degree-2 class is `{ v(f)>=-3, f_{-3} odd }`, and
   125	
   126	```text
   127	v(f) >= 0   <=>   f in C[Z]  (i.e. Q=0 and deg_A P = 0).            (2.4)
   128	```
   129	
   130	*Proof.* `P=sum_i C_i(Z)A^i` maps to `sum_i C_i(V^2-T)T^-i`, whose lowest
   131	`T`-power is `T^{-deg_A P}` with coefficient `C_{deg_A P}(V^2) != 0`; likewise
   132	`UQ` maps to `V sum_j D_j(V^2-T) T^{-j-1}`, lowest power `T^{-1-deg_A Q}` with
   133	coefficient `V D_{deg_A Q}(V^2)`. The even/odd parts cannot cancel each other,
   134	so `v(f)=min` of the two. For the equivalence, `v(f)>=-(d+1)` gives
   135	`deg_A P<=d+1` and `deg_A Q<=d`, and `f_{-(d+1)} = C_{d+1}(V^2)+V D_d(V^2)`
   136	is odd exactly when `C_{d+1}=0`. For (2.4), `v(UQ)>=0` forces `deg_A Q<=-1`,
   137	i.e. `Q=0`. QED
   138	
   139	Two remarks that matter later. First, `S \ Phi = Spec RR[A^-1]` is
   140	`G_m x A^1`: the interior divisor `Phi=V(A,U)` is exactly `T=infinity`, and the
   141	whole `A`-adic tower of the horn lives at the *opposite* end `T=0`. Second, the
   142	constraint `deg_A <= 2` is a bound on the order of the pole of `f` along the
   143	prime divisor `{T=0}` of a compactification of `S \ Phi`, plus one parity
   144	condition on the leading coefficient. Under the source chart `iota` of
   145	`theta:(3.12)` one has `T=x^-2`, `V=x^-1+xy`, `{x,y}=1`; so `v` is (twice) the
   146	monomial divisorial valuation of `C(x,y)` with `v(x)=-1`, `v(y)=+1`, whose
   147	residue field is `C(xy-bar)`. The `A`-filtration of the horn is therefore a
   148	classical filtration at one divisorial place at infinity of the plane. No flag,
   149	place or series is identified here: `{T=0}`, `Phi`, the target place
   150	`q_infty` of `theta:§4`, and the generic ramified factor of `theta:(1.7)`
   151	remain four distinct objects, and none of them is used below.
   152	
   153	## 3. Why the naive leading-term induction breaks at A-degree 2
   154	
   155	The review located a free compensator in `[A^1]O` (`review:§6`). The true
   156	situation is stronger and settles the question of bottom-up induction for good.
   157	
   158	Write `P=sum C_i A^i`, `Q=sum D_i A^i`, `R_0=sum E_i A^i`, `S_0=sum L_i A^i`
   159	with `C_i,D_i,E_i,L_i in C[Z]`, and abbreviate the level-0 residue data
   160	`p=C_0, q=D_0, r=E_0, s=L_0`. Reading off (1.1) term by term in `A` (each
   161	summand of `E` and of `O` is one of `A^2, H=A+A^2Z, c=2+4AZ` times a
   162	bilinear expression, so this is a finite collection of index shifts, not an
   163	expansion) gives the two support laws:
   164	
   165	```text
   166	[A^n]O involves C,E at level <= n+1 and D,L at level <= n;
   167	[A^n]E involves all four at level <= n.
   168	```
   169	
   170	and, isolating in each the part where one index is `n+1` resp. `n`:
   295	### 4.3 Affine normal form and the census
   296	
   297	The target group `Aff_2(C)` acts by `(f,g) -> (af+bg+e, cf+dg+e')`, replacing
   298	`kappa` by `(ad-bc)kappa != 0`. Each of `P,Q,R_0,S_0` transforms `C`-linearly,
   299	so the `A`-degree-2 class is `Aff_2`-stable, and proving
   300	`OBSTRUCTION[A-DEGREE-TWO]` for normalized pairs proves it outright. No residue
   301	normalization is used or needed.
   302	
   303	**Proposition 4.2 (normal form).** Any `A`-degree-2 solution can be moved by
   304	`Aff_2` to one with `alpha > beta >= 1`, hence with
   305	
   306	```text
   307	(alpha,beta) in { (3,2), (3,1), (2,1) }.                              (4.3)
   308	```
   309	
   310	*Proof.* Swap `f,g` so `alpha >= beta`. If `alpha = beta` then (N0) reads
   311	`Wr(f_{-alpha}, g_{-beta}) = 0`, so `g_{-beta} = lambda f_{-alpha}` for a
   312	nonzero constant and `g - lambda f` has strictly smaller pole order while
   313	staying in the class; one such step suffices. Then (4.2) gives `beta >= 1` and
   314	Proposition 2.3 gives `alpha <= 3`. QED
   315	
   316	Three cells. Sections 5 and 6 dispose of two and reduce the third to an
   317	explicit rigid system. Note that (4.3) already forbids `alpha = beta`, which is
   318	the classical "same degree" case; the surviving arithmetic is
   319	`(alpha,beta)=(3,2)` with `gcd = 1`.
   320	
   321	## 5. Cells (3,1) and (2,1) are empty
   322	
   323	Throughout, `beta=1` forces, by Proposition 2.3 read coefficientwise,
   324	
   325	```text
   326	g_{-3}=V L_2(V^2) = 0  =>  L_2=0;
   327	g_{-2}=E_2(V^2)+V L_1(V^2) = 0  =>  E_2=L_1=0;
   328	g_{-1}=E_1(V^2)+V L_0(V^2) != 0.                                       (5.1)
   329	```
   330	
   331	### 5.1 Cell (3,1)
   332	
   333	Here `alpha=3`, so `f_{-3}` is odd and nonzero, and (4.1) with `k0=1` gives
   334	`f_{-3}=c' k^3` where `k := g_{-1}`. By the parity transfer of §4.1, `k` is
   335	odd, so by (5.1) `E_1=0`. Hence
   336	
   337	```text
   338	R_0=r(Z),  S_0=s(Z),   i.e. g has A-degree 0, and k = V s(V^2).       (5.2)
   339	```
   340	
   341	Consequently `g^2=(r^2+Hs^2)+U(2rs)` and `g^3=(r^3+3Hrs^2)+U(3r^2s+Hs^3)`
   342	both lie in the `A`-degree-2 class, because `deg_A H = 2`. Now read (2.3) at
   343	`m=-3`, where only `(i,j)=(-3,0)` and `(-2,-1)` occur:
   344	
   345	```text
   346	3 f_{-3} g_0' - f_{-2}' g_{-1} + 2 f_{-2} g_{-1}' = 0.
   347	```
   348	
   349	Since `f_{-2}' k - 2 f_{-2} k' = k^3 (f_{-2} k^{-2})'`, this integrates once:
   350	
   351	```text
   352	f_{-2} = k^2 ( 3c' g_0 + c_3 ),      c_3 in C.                        (5.3)
   353	```
   354	
   355	But `(g^3)_{-3}=k^3`, `(g^3)_{-2}=3k^2 g_0` and `(g^2)_{-2}=k^2`, so (5.3) says
   356	exactly that
   357	
   358	```text
   359	ft := f - c' g^3 - c_3 g^2   satisfies  v(ft) >= -1,
   360	```
   361	
   362	while `ft` is still in the `A`-degree-2 class and `{ft,g}={f,g}=kappa`. If
   363	`v(ft)=-1` then `alpha=beta=1` and one more `Aff_2` step (Proposition 4.2)
   364	gives `fh` with `v(fh)>=0`; by (2.4), `fh in C[Z]`. Lemma 4.1 now contradicts
   365	`{fh,g}=kappa`. Cell `(3,1)` is empty.
   366	
   367	### 5.2 Cell (2,1)
   368	
   369	Here `alpha=2`, so `D_2=0`, `f_{-3}=0` and
   370	`f_{-2}=C_2(V^2)+V D_1(V^2) != 0`. Equation (4.1) with `(alpha,beta)=(2,1)`
   371	gives `f_{-2}=c_0 k^2`, `k=g_{-1}`. No parity is available here (a square
   372	carries none), so `E_1` need not vanish; but by (5.1)
   373	`R_0=r+A E_1`, `S_0=s`, whence
   374	
   375	```text
   376	g^2 = ( (r+A E_1)^2 + H s^2 ) + U ( 2 (r+A E_1) s ),
   377	```
   378	
   379	whose four coefficient polynomials have `A`-degrees `2` and `1`. So `g^2` is in
   380	the class, and `ft := f - c_0 g^2` has `v(ft) >= -1`, is in the class, and
   381	satisfies `{ft,g}=kappa`. Exactly as in §5.1, one further `Aff_2` step and
   382	(2.4) produce `fh in C[Z]` with `{fh,g}=kappa`, contradicting Lemma 4.1. Cell
   383	`(2,1)` is empty.
   384	
   385	### 5.3 What the two kills share
   386	
   387	Both use the same three-step pattern, which is the promotable technique of this
   388	report: (i) the leading-form equation (N0) identifies `f_{-alpha}` with a power
   389	of `g_{-beta}`; (ii) because `beta | alpha`, that power is realized by an actual
   390	element `g^{alpha/beta}` of `RR` which happens to stay inside the `A`-degree-2
   391	class -- this is where the truncation is exploited, and it is exactly the step
   392	Section 3 shows can never be done from the bottom; (iii) subtracting it is a
   393	*nonlinear* automorphism of the constraint (`{g^k,g}=0`), so the pole order
   394	drops without touching `kappa`, and the descent terminates on Lemma 4.1.
   395	
   396	Step (ii) is available precisely when `beta` divides `alpha`. This is why the
   397	census (4.3) has exactly one survivor.
   398	
   399	## 6. The surviving cell (3,2)
   400	
   401	### 6.1 Rigid structure forced by the leading forms
   402	
   403	`beta=2` gives `L_2=0` and `g_{-2}=E_2(V^2)+V L_1(V^2) != 0`. `alpha=3` gives
   404	`f_{-3}=V D_2(V^2)` odd and nonzero. With `gcd(3,2)=1`, (4.1) reads
   405	
   406	```text
   407	f_{-3}=c_1 h^3,      g_{-2}=c_2 h^2,      c_1 c_2 != 0,
   408	```
   409	
   410	and the parity transfer makes `h` odd, say `h=V eta(V^2)` with
   411	`eta in C[Z]`. Then `h^2` is even, so `g_{-2}` is even, so `L_1=0`. Comparing
   412	coefficients (`V^2 = W`, then rename `W` to `Z`):
   413	
   414	```text
   415	S_0 = s(Z)                     (g has odd part of A-degree 0),
   416	E_2 = c_2 Z eta^2,   D_2 = c_1 Z eta^3,   equivalently  Z D_2^2 = mu E_2^3
   417	with mu=c_1^2/c_2^3.                                                   (6.1)
   418	```
   419	
   420	Three consequences worth isolating: `deg_A S_0 = 0`; `E_2` is `Z` times a
   421	square and `D_2` is `Z` times a cube; and in particular `Z` divides both. The
   422	relation `Z D_2^2 = mu E_2^3` is the exact "second-order invariant" for this
   423	cell -- it is the resultant-free integrated form of the leading system and it
   424	is not absorbable, because there is no level-3 datum.
   425	
   426	Control. With `S_0=s`, the four summands of (1.1) cap at `deg_A O <= 3` and
   427	`deg_A E <= 5`. Reading off `[A^5]E` gives
   428	`-D_2 E_2 + 3Z D_2 E_2' - 2Z D_2' E_2 = 0`, and substituting (6.1) makes every
   429	term cancel identically. So `[A^5]E` is *equivalent* to (N0). I did this
   430	substitution by hand in both directions; it is a nontrivial check that the
   431	`(T,V)` machinery of Section 2 and the direct `A`-expansion of (1.1) agree.
   432	
   433	### 6.2 The next two structural equations
   434	
   435	`[A^3]O` is, after dividing by `4`,
   436	
   437	```text
   438	2 Wr(C_2,E_2) + D_2 s + 3Z D_2 s' - Z D_2' s = 0,   Wr(u,v)=uv'-u'v.  (6.2)
   439	```
   440	
   441	Substituting (6.1) turns (6.2) into a first-order linear equation for `C_2`
   442	whose homogeneous solution is proportional to `E_2`; integrating it once gives
   443	the complete solution
   444	
   445	```text
   446	2 c_2 C_2 = Z eta ( 3 c_1 s + c_5 eta ),      c_5 in C.               (6.3)
   447	```
   448	
   449	So `C_2` is *determined* by the pair `(eta,s)` up to a single constant, and
   450	`Z | C_2`. Together with `L_2=0` this says the whole level-2 datum
   451	`(C_2,D_2,E_2,L_2)` vanishes at `Z=0` and is a function of `(eta,s,c_1,c_2,c_5)`
   452	alone.
   453	
   454	`[A^4]E` is the first equation reaching level 1. Writing
   455	`G := E_1-E_2'` and `F := D_1-D_2'` (these are exactly the `T`-coefficients
   456	`g^{ev}_{-1}` and `f^{od}_{-2}` of Section 2, which is why they group), the
   457	equation collapses to
   458	
   459	```text
   460	3 c_1 eta ( 2Z G' eta - G eta - 2Z G eta' )
   461	   = 2 c_2 ( 2Z F' eta - 3F eta - 8Z F eta' ).                        (6.4)
   462	```
   463	
   464	**Proposition 6.1 (a parity-of-degree rigidity).** In (6.4), put
   465	`e=deg eta`, `gamma=deg G`, `phi=deg F`. Every term on the left has degree
   466	`gamma+2e` with combined leading coefficient
   467	`3c_1 eta_e^2 G_gamma (2 gamma - 1 - 2e)`, and every term on the right has
   468	degree `phi+e` with combined leading coefficient
   469	`2 c_2 eta_e F_phi (2 phi - 3 - 8e)`. Both bracketed integers are **odd**,
   470	hence nonzero. Therefore either
   471	
   472	```text
   473	F = G = 0,     i.e.  E_1 = E_2',  D_1 = D_2',
   474	```
   475	
   476	or `F,G` are both nonzero and `phi = gamma + e`, with the leading-coefficient
   477	relation `3 c_1 eta_e G_gamma (2 gamma - 2e - 1) = 2 c_2 F_phi (2 gamma - 6e - 3)`.
   478	
   479	This is the exact analogue, one level up, of the mechanism `theta:(3.9)-(3.10)`
   480	that closed the `A`-linear case: a degree balance forced by a parity that no
   481	choice of coefficients can break. It does not yet close the cell, because there
   482	are still compensators available at levels 0 and 1.
   483	
   484	### 6.3 What remains, exactly
   485	
   486	After (6.1), (6.3), and Proposition 6.1, the free data of cell `(3,2)` is
   487	
   488	```text
   489	eta, s, C_0, C_1, D_0=q, D_1, E_0=r, E_1  in C[Z];   c_1,c_2,c_5,kappa in C,
   490	```
   491	
   492	with `c_1 c_2 kappa != 0` and `eta != 0`, and the remaining equations are
   493	
   494	```text
   495	[A^n]O = 0  for n = 0,1,2;      [A^n]E = 0 for n = 1,2,3;   [A^0]E = kappa,
   496	```
   497	
   498	i.e. seven equations, `[A^3]O`, `[A^4]E`, `[A^5]E` having been consumed above.
   499	This is not a dimension-count kill: eight unknown polynomials against seven
   500	equations, all of unbounded degree. `[A^0]O` and `[A^0]E` are the promoted
   501	residue relations `4(C_1 r' - p' E_1) = 2(q's-qs')` and `2(qr'-p's)=kappa`;
   502	`[A^1]O` is the equation carrying the free Bezout compensator
   503	`8(C_2 r' - p' E_2)` of Section 3, now with `C_2, E_2` already pinned by (6.1)
   504	and (6.3), so it is no longer free -- it has become a genuine constraint on
   505	`(eta,s,C_1,E_1,q,r,p)`. That reversal is the structural gain of this section
   506	and is where a closing argument should be sought.
   507	
   508	I did not close cell `(3,2)` inside the budget. Per the fail-closed rule it is
   509	typed `OPEN` in Section 7 and specified for exact computation in Section 8. No
   510	consistent stratum was found; nothing here is a counterexample cell.
